@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About | It's About Bloody Time</PageTitle>

<section class="event-page information-page about-page">
    <div class="page-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the archive</p>
        <h1>It's About Bloody Time</h1>
        <p class="page-introduction">A simple place to collect, organise, and explore the events that make a story worth telling.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="information-content about-guide">
        <section class="information-section about-section">
            <h2>See the bigger picture</h2>
            <p>History is rarely a collection of isolated dates. A single event can be the result of years of preparation, a turning point in a larger story, or the beginning of consequences that continue long afterwards.</p>
            <p>It's About Bloody Time brings those moments together in one place, so a subject can be explored as a connected story rather than a list of facts.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="information-section about-section">
            <h2>Follow events in chronological order</h2>
            <p>Every event has a start date and an end date. When the timeline is rendered, those dates place events in their true order and show how long they lasted. A one-day discovery, a decade-long project, and a period of social change can all sit together on the same visual record.</p>
            <p>Seeing the sequence makes it easier to recognise what happened first, what overlapped, and which developments may have influenced what came next.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="information-section about-section">
            <h2>Understand how moments relate</h2>
            <p>A title identifies the moment, a short summary makes it easy to scan, and a detailed description preserves the context behind it. Together, these details help reveal the relationships between people, places, decisions, discoveries, and outcomes.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Trace the build-up to a major event.</li>
                <li>Compare events happening at the same time.</li>
                <li>Follow an idea, person, organisation, or place across many years.</li>
                <li>Keep the evidence and context that future readers need.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="information-section about-section">
            <h2>Create an archive you can return to</h2>
            <p>Build timelines for world history, local stories, family milestones, research projects, or any subject that benefits from a clearer sense of time. Add images when they help bring a moment to life, then revisit the rendered timeline whenever you want to understand the shape of the story again.</p>
        </section>
    </div>
</section>
